1
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
3 days of peace, music...and love.

2
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Woodstock is a documentary, history, music film directed by Michael Wadleigh.

3
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:06,000
An intimate look at the Woodstock Music & Art Festival held in Bethel, NY in 1969, from preparation through cleanup, with historic access to insiders, blistering concert footage, and portraits of the concertgoers; negative and positive aspects are shown,
from drug use by performers to naked fans sliding in the mud, from the collapse of the fences by the unexpected hordes to the surreal arrival of National Guard helicopters with food and medical assistance for the impromptu city of 500,000.

4
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:26,000
Self, played by Richie Havens.

5
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:46,000
Self, played by Joan Baez.

6
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:06,000
Self - The Who, played by Roger Daltrey.

7
00:58:20,000 --> 00:58:26,000
Self - The Who, played by John Entwistle.

8
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:46,000
Self - The Who, played by Keith Moon.

9
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
Self - The Who, played by Pete Townshend.

10
01:23:20,000 --> 01:23:26,000
Woodstock is a 1970 American documentary film of the watershed counterculture
Woodstock Festival which took place in August 1969 near Bethel, New York.

11
01:31:40,000 --> 01:31:46,000
The film was directed by Michael Wadleigh in his directorial debut.

12
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:06,000
Seven editors are credited, including Thelma
Schoonmaker, Martin Scorsese, and Wadleigh.

13
01:48:20,000 --> 01:48:26,000
Woodstock was a great commercial and critical success.

14
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:46,000
It received the Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature.

15
02:05:00,000 --> 02:05:06,000
Schoonmaker was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Film
Editing, a rare distinction for a documentary.

16
02:13:20,000 --> 02:13:26,000
Dan Wallin and L.

17
02:21:40,000 --> 02:21:46,000
Johnson were nominated for the Academy Award for Best Sound.

18
02:30:00,000 --> 02:30:06,000
The film was screened at the 1970 Cannes Film Festival, but
was not entered into the main competition.

19
02:38:20,000 --> 02:38:26,000
The 1970 theatrical release of the film ran 185 minutes.

20
02:46:40,000 --> 02:46:46,000
A director's cut spanning 224 minutes was released in 1994.

21
02:55:00,000 --> 02:55:06,000
Both cuts take liberties with the timeline of the festival.

22
03:03:20,000 --> 03:03:26,000
However, the opening and closing acts are the same in the film as they appeared on
stage; Richie Havens opens the show and Jimi Hendrix closes it.

23
03:11:40,000 --> 03:11:46,000
In 1996, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

24
03:20:00,000 --> 03:20:06,000
An expanded 40th Anniversary Edition of Woodstock, released on June 9, 2009, in Blu-ray and DVD formats, features additional performances not
before seen in the film, and it includes lengthened versions of existing performances, such as Creedence Clearwater Revival.

25
03:28:20,000 --> 03:28:26,000
The film explores themes of hippie, free love,
music festival, marijuana, rock music.

26
03:36:40,000 --> 03:36:46,000
The tone is uplifting and mind_bending.
